%S A399133 1,2,4,6,12,24,36,48,60,120,180,240,360,720,840,1260,1680,2520,5040,
%T A399133 10080,15120,25200,27720,55440,110880,166320,277200,332640,554400,
%U A399133 720720,1441440,2162160,3603600,4324320,7207200,8648640,10810800,21621600,36756720,43243200,61261200,73513440
%N A399133 Superior factor-dense numbers: where records are set for d(k)/exp(log(k)/(1+log(1+log(k)))), where d(k) = A000005(k), the number of divisors of k.
%C A399133 All these numbers are highly composite (A002182) and even factor-dense (A210594) under the expanded definition of factor-density given in the comments. However, factor-density appears to coincide with being highly composite other than at 45360. In contrast, the number of divisors is required to grow more quickly with this sequence, which omits many highly composite and even factor-dense numbers, starting with 7560. I do not know if this sequence is infinite.
%H A399133 Hal M. Switkay, <a href="/A399133/b399133.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..59</a>
%e A399133 60 is in this sequence, because 60 has 12 divisors, and 12/exp(log(60)/(1+log(1+log(60)))) (approximately 2.527) is larger than the corresponding quotients for numbers smaller than 60.
%e A399133 7560 is not in this sequence, despite being a highly composite number with 64 divisors, and indeed factor-dense. Its quotient is approximately 4.259, smaller than 4.368, the quotient for 5040, which has 60 divisors.
%t A399133 fd[k_]:=DivisorSigma[0,k]/Exp[Log[k]/(1+Log[1+Log[k]])];s={1};sd={fd[1]};k=1;Do[Until[fd[k]>sd[[-1]],k++];AppendTo[s,k];AppendTo[sd,fd[k]],{n,26}];s (* _James C. McMahon_, Aug 26 2026 *)
%Y A399133 Cf. A000005, A002182, A210594.
%K A399133 nonn,new
%O A399133 1,2
%A A399133 _Hal M. Switkay_, Aug 19 2026
